and sports secretary at Grant Shapps  joining us at from just down the road on  Abington green at minister very good  morning to you and you'll have heard  that breaking news in just the past  couple of minutes the statement from  easyJet that they could no be grounding  the majority of their fleet yeah that's  right I seen that statement look I'm  meeting with the whole of the transport  sector this week but mainly to feed into  an economic and business committee  chaired by the Chancellor in order that  we can do what we've said all along  which is we understand this is a crisis  like we haven't seen before where  businesses which are otherwise  sustainable otherwise good businesses of  course should be able to survive through  this this situation and that's what  we're going to be trying to put into  place for all businesses and individuals  in this country as we join this great  national effort to defeat this virus we  know that even before the statement from  easyJet that they are going to be  canceling a number of flights that the  war noisy it's coming from the aviation  industry that that may they might be  requiring a bailout of sorts are you  minded to provide it well we know that  aviation of course is at the forefront  in many ways because the first thing  that stopped is travel internationally  in particular but actually we want to  you know make sure that as I said you  know companies and and individuals  organizations who are in a good state  not those that we're going to fail  anyway but the good state are able to  continue and and so we're be looking at  all of these measures I'll be discussing  them with the Chancellor and the prime  minister later today in those  discussions with the sector are ongoing  including summits I've got in transport  with railroad Aviation Hall years later  today and later this week it sounds very  much like bailing out the airlines is an  option that remains on the table for now  at least well there are lots of  different options here including some  other things that people have been  looking for for example HMRC offer a  time  pay there's obviously issues over the  way that various different taxes  regulations fall last week we before I  was very active in ensuring that  Airlines weren't having to pointlessly  fly what I was calling ghost planes  empty half-empty fully empty because  there was a slot allocation rule at  airports and I lobbied the European  Commission to change that and indeed  they have changed it so there's lots of  different ways to assist but this is now  a great national effort that every  organization every individual every  citizen in this country and indeed  around the world is having to join in  order to make sure that we can and will  in the end defeat this virus I wonder if  we might speak more generally about the  government's response to Corvette 19 and  many people will welcome the  announcement that there are going to be  daily televised briefings from the  government we will talk in and more  detail about the government's  communication strategy in just a second  but many people watching this will be of  one mind with one big question in their  head what is it that the government  knows that the CM or the CS or the Prime  Minister knows that means that I will  approach to tackling corvid 19 is  markedly different from pretty much the  rest of the world  well I think that if you are looking for  this sort of a uniqueness of the British  approach it would simply be those  initials you mentioned the chief medical  officer the chief scientific officer it  is a real desire to be driven by the  evidence and by the science now I don't  think that necessarily means that our  approach is markedly different as you  say but I do think it means that we  deploy each of these different measures  at the appropriate time and there'll be  another Cobra planning session later  today the Prime Minister will chair that  I'll be at will we'll be discussing all  the things that were in that overall  action plan issued near the start of  this crisis so we are just being  entirely science led we're not doing the  things that you know sometimes perhaps  are are happening elsewhere just because  it seems like a popular thing to do we  want to know that the scientists back in  and that's I suppose the hallmark of  of this country's response but the  accusation that is being made is that  the government is reacting in some cases  in some ways more slowly than European  partners the United States you'll have  heard the news about Los Angeles and and  New York simply because the Health  Service cannot cope as it stands figures  from the NHS released in January show  that in terms of critical care we're  needing capacity even before covered 19  all over 4,000 adult critical care beds  three and a half thousand of them are  occupied 312 pediatric intensive care  beds 247 of them occupied and all that  before we expand the system try to hire  new intensive care nurses I mean how  confident are you that there is the  capacity to to treat people what I think  surprise any of any of your viewers that  of course something like this at the  scale of this virus is an enormous  capacity challenge for any health system  throughout the world it doesn't matter  where you are but just because of the  size and scale of it and of course it is  also the case that it makes a lot of  sense to ensure that we do things to try  to manage the pressures on the on the  health service but the actual  decision-making as you've seen and  you've seen the Prime Minister you'll  see it again today flanked by those  medical and scientific experts every  single decision that we've made has been  based around what does the science say  what's the medical evidence and we're  going to be actually publishing all the  background evidence and of course it's  quite right that some people will have  different opinions and really we want to  hear those opinions because it's very  very important that we get this right  but we are making every single possible  effort to do that and doing in a  completely transparent way I would say  the UK is probably just been at a  slightly different stage obviously the  comparable places like Italy but also a  little behind where France and Germany  are it's not that we're not going to get  there but of course our responses are  timed in a different way unique to the  particular stage of this that we're in  in the UK but but at this stage in the  spread of corvid 19 we have the health  secretary issuing a call for help from  industry to expand  of ventilators available to the NHS we  spoke over the weekend to one person who  didn't think that was necessarily  practicable let's just have a listen the  biggest providers are in America guess  where they're going to supply the next  biggest of strangely enough is New  Zealand and they're looking after the  Southeast Asia the rest of Europe and of  course guess where the European  manufacturers are going to sell their  stuff to because you know it's to Europe  and we're not in Europe anymore what  information have you had that suggests  that we will be able to mass-produce  ventilators in the coming weeks first of  all we've been buying ventilators over  the last few weeks and months so that's  been an ongoing process secondly we are  in Europe still that we don't physically  geographically move and if you were  listening to some other radio broadcast  this morning you had no chief execs very  large manufacturing advanced  manufacturing organizations who very  much up for the manufacturing of  ventilators in fact I've had two  approaches from constituents companies  advanced manufacturers this weekend and  today there'll be a phone number coming  live today and an email address for  people who want to offer manufacturing  assistance that would be a base as a  business department number that will  come available I think this is all part  of sort of this great national effort to  work together and do everything we  possibly had as a nation to solve this  problem and make sure that we protect  the elderly and the vulnerable in our  society who are the people who are most  likely going to be the ones who would  need the ventilators themselves and I  wonder if we can talk about the way in  which the government has been  communicating its strategy to the wider  public there has been some confusion we  had the chief scientific officer at  talking about Harrod immunity then the  health secretary saying it wasn't part  of the strategy you had the the prime  minister in his press briefing at saying  that we didn't need to ban mass  gatherings then briefing to the press  that that was a strategy going forward  do you think the government has been in  command of communications actually I  mean the action plan published right at  the beginning actually outlines  virtually all these things so you know  things like home isolation and you know  households staying at home even elderly  and vulnerable people I'm staying at  home all of those things are already  outlined in the action plan the question  really I guess this is what you're  coming to is really that of timing and  when each of these different elements  come in and again we will be driven by  the scientific advice on this which I  think's absolutely the right approach  you can never over communicate I  entirely agree with that but I do think  that it's important that we do not get  knocked off the course  of what this country is because I'm very  well so far which is to follow the  medical and scientific advice rather  than doing things which just sound good  but perhaps don't have the right impact  at the right moment in time why then did  the government announced that millions  of people over the age of 70 may have to  be isolated for as much as four months  why did they announce that through an  anonymous briefing to a political editor  a political journalist and not the Prime  Minister standing at a lectern or  yourself or the health secretary  speaking to the public directly why is  public health policy being given to the  public  via anonymous briefing that's just not  true if you look at the action plan  which was published at the beginning of  this it's literally written in there so  it's just not true  about that accusation but also I mean I  would say it's useful before we're  actually asking over 70s to isolate my  parents for example in their their 80s  my father 88 I was speaking to them this  weekend and helping them to set up an  online shopping account so they can get  their groceries delivered and it's  practical and helpful for people to know  what's coming down the line which is why  it is written in the action plan itself  in order that they can get themselves  prepared and my mother for example  hasn't had the opportunity to read the  action plan my mother discovered that  she may very well have to spend four  months in her own home away from her  family through an anonymous briefing to  a political journalist now we heard from  the the health secretary at the weekend  at the following let's just have quick  listen to this  but just be crystal-clear on the elderly  the government will be asking the over  70s to self isolate potentially for a  period of months at some point in the  future that is in the action plan yes  and we'll be setting it out with more  detail when that's the right it's the  right time to do so because we  absolutely appreciate that it is that is  a very big ask of the elderly and the  vulnerable and it's for their own self  protection  made two absolutely clear that he does  not feel that that was the right time to  talk about it the right time to provide  detail on that particular piece of  public policy a large part of the  strategy instead emerged on a blog on a  Facebook post written by a political  journalist and not from elect and so  again I ask do you think that the  government is completely in control of  the way in which it is informing the  public like afraid I can't be in control  of every blog that a political  journalist decides to write but what I  do know is that as the health sector  just said it's actually in the action  plan which was widely covered by the  newspapers at the time including that  point and I think it is useful for you  and me people with elderly parents and  relatives to be able to prepare them and  this is what it is I mean I can't tell  you otherwise this is a big national  effort required for a international  crisis and people will need to prepare  in that way that's why we're moving to  daily press conferences from today as  well but our approach is to be  completely open with it and publish all  the information all the background and  try to give people the best possible  advice as we pull together as a nation  to defeat this thing

